  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Time Scaling",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a known Fourier transform pair for $x(t)$ into the transform of $x(at)$ by applying reciprocal frequency scaling and the 1/|a| magnitude factor.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Scaling",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If you already know a pair like $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, time scaling tells you how to reuse it when the time variable is multiplied by a nonzero factor. The move is to keep the same spectrum shape, substitute $\\omega/a$ for $\\omega$, and carry the $1/|a|$ factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with $a=2$. This is the simplest compression case: $x(2t)$ runs twice as fast in time, so its spectrum widens on the frequency axis and drops to half height.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(at) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{|a|}X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the concrete result is $x(2t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac12 X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{2}\\right)$. The reciprocal frequency substitution is the same rule in every case; only the scale factor changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same rule on the expansion case $x(t/2)$. With $a=\\frac12$, the spectrum becomes $2X(j2\\omega)$, which confirms that stretching time compresses frequency while doubling the amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming $x(2t)$ from a Known Fourier Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $x(2t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is the scale factor $a=2$ multiplying time. Start from the known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and treat $x(2t)$ as the same signal with time compressed by a factor of 2.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the time-scaling rule $x(at) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{|a|}X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right)$ with $a=2$. The frequency variable becomes $\\omega/2$, and the spectrum picks up the factor $1/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(2t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac12 X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transform of $x(2t)$ is $\\frac12 X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{2}\\right)$. Compression in time widens the spectrum and lowers its height by one-half.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Transforming $x(t/2)$ from a Known Fourier Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $x(t/2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is the expansion factor hidden in $x(t/2)$. Start from the known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and treat $x(t/2)$ as the same signal with time stretched by a factor of 2.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the time-scaling rule $x(at) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{|a|}X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right)$ with $a=\\frac12$. The frequency variable becomes $\\omega/(1/2)=2\\omega$, and the spectrum picks up the factor $1/|1/2|=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t/2) \\leftrightarrow 2X(j2\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transform of $x(t/2)$ is $2X(j2\\omega)$. Stretching time compresses the spectrum on the frequency axis and doubles its height.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Applying the 1/|a| Magnitude Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $3x(2t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is the outside scalar $3$ multiplying a time-scaled signal. Start from the known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, separate the amplitude factor from the time scaling, and handle $3x(2t)$ in two steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First apply the time-scaling rule to $x(2t)$ with $a=2$. That gives $\\frac12 X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{2}\\right)$, and the scalar $3$ still sits outside the transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3x(2t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{3}{2}X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the final transform is $\\frac{3}{2}X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{2}\\right)$. The scalar multiplies the spectrum after time scaling, so the coefficient and the reciprocal factor combine cleanly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Time Scaling",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Using Time Scaling with a General Scale Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and a nonzero scale factor $a$, state the Fourier transform of $x(at)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the general nonzero scale factor $a$. Start from the known pair and keep the scale symbol explicit so the result works for every nonzero value of $a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $x(at)$, replace $\\omega$ by $\\omega/a$ in the transform argument and attach the magnitude factor $1/|a|$. The absolute value keeps the scaling correct whether $a$ is positive or negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(at) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{|a|}X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the general time-scaling rule is $x(at) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{|a|}X\\left(j\\frac{\\omega}{a}\\right)$. This one formula contains the earlier numeric cases and is the version to reuse in later work.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
